Programming in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>3  Decisions and loops</H1>
<HR>
So far your programs have carried out every statement once, from top to bottom. Real programs need to make
choices - do this if that is true - and to repeat things. In this chapter you will learn how, and write a
times table program, the counting game FizzBuzz, and a guessing game.<P>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>True and false</H2>
Besides numbers and strings, JavaScript has two more values: <CODE>true</CODE> and <CODE>false</CODE>. They
are called <I>booleans</I>, after the mathematician George Boole. You get them by comparing things:<P>
<PRE>
print(3 &lt; 5);          // true
print(3 &gt; 5);          // false
print(2 + 2 === 4);    // true
</PRE>
These are the comparison operators:<P>
<PRE>
Operator  Meaning                      Example        Result
  ===     is equal to                  5 === 5        true
  !==     is not equal to              5 !== 5        false
   &lt;      is less than                 3 &lt; 5          true
   &gt;      is greater than              3 &gt; 5          false
   &lt;=     is less than or equal to     5 &lt;= 5         true
   &gt;=     is greater than or equal to  3 &gt;= 5         false
</PRE>
"Is equal to" is written with three equals signs. One equals sign already means "becomes", as you saw in
chapter 2, so it can't also mean "is equal to".<P>
<B>Note</B>: JavaScript also has <CODE>==</CODE> and <CODE>!=</CODE>, with two equals signs. They try to be
helpful by converting values before comparing them, so <CODE>'1' == 1</CODE> is true, and so, less
helpfully, is <CODE>0 == ''</CODE>. The rules are complicated and a common source of mistakes. Always use
<CODE>===</CODE> and <CODE>!==</CODE>, which compare values exactly as they are: <CODE>'1' === 1</CODE> is
false, because a string is never equal to a number.<P>
Strings can be compared too. <CODE>===</CODE> checks whether two strings are exactly the same, capital
letters and all, so <CODE>'yes' === 'Yes'</CODE> is false.<P>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>Making decisions: if</H2>
An <CODE>if</CODE> statement runs some statements only when something is true:<P>
<PRE>
const age = Number(await input('How old are you? '));
if (age &gt;= 18) {
  print('You can vote.');
}
print('Thank you.');
</PRE>
The comparison goes in brackets after <CODE>if</CODE>, and the statements to run go between curly brackets,
<CODE>{</CODE> and <CODE>}</CODE>. Statements between curly brackets are called a <I>block</I>. If the
comparison is true, the block runs; if not, it is skipped. Either way the program carries on after the
closing bracket, so <I>Thank you.</I> is always printed.<P>
The statements inside the block are indented - moved in by two spaces - so you can see at a glance which
statements belong to the <CODE>if</CODE>. JavaScript doesn't mind how you lay the program out, but people
do. <B>Note</B>: the Tab key in Edit moves the caret along to line up with the next word on the line above, so
when the line above is indented, Tab takes you to the same indent. Or just type the spaces. Appendix D has
more about Tab in Edit.<P>
<H3>else</H3>
Add <CODE>else</CODE> and a second block to say what to do when the comparison is false:<P>
<PRE>
if (age &gt;= 18) {
  print('You can vote.');
} else {
  print(`You can vote in ${18 - age} years.`);
}
</PRE>
Exactly one of the two blocks runs. For more than two choices, chain them with <CODE>else if</CODE>:<P>
<PRE>
if (age &lt; 13) {
  print('Child');
} else if (age &lt; 20) {
  print('Teenager');
} else {
  print('Adult');
}
</PRE>
JavaScript tries each comparison in turn, from the top, and runs the block of the first one that is true.
The rest are skipped. The last <CODE>else</CODE> catches anything that got past all the others.<P>
<H3>And, or, not</H3>
You can combine comparisons:<P>
<PRE>
Operator  Meaning   True when ...
   &amp;&amp;     and       both sides are true
   ||     or        either side is true, or both
   !      not       the value after it is false
</PRE>
For example:<P>
<PRE>
if (age &gt;= 13 &amp;&amp; age &lt;= 19) {
  print('Teenager');
}
if (answer === 'y' || answer === 'yes') {
  print('OK');
}
</PRE>
Notice that each side of <CODE>&amp;&amp;</CODE> and <CODE>||</CODE> has to be a complete comparison:
<CODE>answer === 'y' || 'yes'</CODE> doesn't do what it seems to say.<P>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>Repeating things: for</H2>
Computers are good at doing the same thing many times. A <CODE>for</CODE> loop repeats a block with a
counter that goes up each time round:<P>
<PRE>
for (let i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++) {
  print(`This is time number ${i}`);
}
</PRE>
This prints five lines, numbered 1 to 5. The brackets after <CODE>for</CODE> hold three parts, separated by
semicolons:<P>
<DL>
<DT><CODE>let i = 1</CODE>
<DD>makes the counter, a variable called <CODE>i</CODE>, and starts it at 1. This is done once, before
the loop starts.<P>
<DT><CODE>i &lt;= 5</CODE>
<DD>is checked before each time round. While it is true the block runs; as soon as it is false the loop
stops and the program carries on after it.<P>
<DT><CODE>i++</CODE>
<DD>is done after each time round. <CODE>i++</CODE> is a short way of writing <CODE>i = i + 1</CODE>: it
adds one to <CODE>i</CODE>.<P>
</DL>
Counters are traditionally called <CODE>i</CODE> (for "index"), but any name will do.<P>
Here is a program that prints a times table:<P>
<PRE>
// Tables - a times table
const n = Number(await input('Which times table? '));

if (Number.isNaN(n)) {
  print(&quot;That isn't a number.&quot;);
} else {
  for (let i = 1; i &lt;= 12; i++) {
    print(`${i} x ${n} = ${i * n}`);
  }
}
</PRE>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/Tables" WIDTH=586 HEIGHT=349></CENTER>
Before printing the table, the program checks that you really typed a number. <CODE>Number.isNaN(n)</CODE>
is true if <CODE>n</CODE> is <CODE>NaN</CODE>, the not-a-number value from chapter 2. (You can't check with
<CODE>n === NaN</CODE>: oddly, <CODE>NaN</CODE> isn't equal to anything, not even itself.) The double quotes
around <CODE>"That isn't a number."</CODE> are there because the string has an apostrophe in it.<P>
A block can hold any statements, including other <CODE>if</CODE> statements and loops, so the whole loop sits
inside the <CODE>else</CODE> block. Each extra level is indented by another two spaces.<P>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>FizzBuzz</H2>
FizzBuzz is a counting game. Players count in turn, but say "Fizz" instead of any number that divides by 3,
"Buzz" instead of any number that divides by 5, and "FizzBuzz" for numbers that divide by both. It is also
a famous little programming puzzle, because it needs a loop and a decision together.<P>
<PRE>
// FizzBuzz - the counting game, from 1 to 30
let line = '';
for (let i = 1; i &lt;= 30; i++) {
  let word;
  if (i % 3 === 0 &amp;&amp; i % 5 === 0) {
    word = 'FizzBuzz';
  } else if (i % 3 === 0) {
    word = 'Fizz';
  } else if (i % 5 === 0) {
    word = 'Buzz';
  } else {
    word = String(i);
  }
  line = line + word + ' ';
  if (i % 10 === 0) {
    print(line);
    line = '';
  }
}
</PRE>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/FizzBuzz" WIDTH=586 HEIGHT=349></CENTER>
How does the program know whether a number divides by 3? It uses the remainder operator from chapter 2:
<CODE>i % 3</CODE> is the remainder when <CODE>i</CODE> is divided by 3, and a number divides exactly when
the remainder is 0.<P>
The order of the tests matters. 15 divides by 3, so if the test for 3 came first, 15 would be "Fizz". The
test for both comes first, so that it gets the first chance.<P>
There are two new things in this program.<P>
<CODE>let word;</CODE> makes a variable without giving it a value yet. Until it is given one, its value is
<CODE>undefined</CODE>, JavaScript's way of saying "nothing here". Each branch of the <CODE>if</CODE> then
gives <CODE>word</CODE> a value. <CODE>String(i)</CODE> turns a number into a string, the opposite of
<CODE>Number</CODE>.<P>
Instead of printing each word on its own line, which would fill the window, the program builds up a line
at a time in <CODE>line</CODE>, and prints it after every ten numbers - when <CODE>i % 10</CODE> is 0 -
before starting again with an empty string.<P>
Notice also that a variable made inside a block only exists inside it. <CODE>word</CODE> is made afresh
each time round the loop. <CODE>line</CODE> has to last from one time round to the next, so it is made
before the loop starts.<P>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>Repeating until something happens: while</H2>
A <CODE>for</CODE> loop is best when you know how many times to go round. When you don't, use a
<CODE>while</CODE> loop, which repeats its block for as long as a comparison is true:<P>
<PRE>
let answer = '';
while (answer !== 'yes') {
  answer = await input('Are we there yet? ');
}
print('Hooray!');
</PRE>
<H3>Random numbers</H3>
Games need some luck. <CODE>Math.random()</CODE> gives a different random number each time you call it,
somewhere between 0 and 1 (it can be 0, but it is always less than 1), such as 0.7342. To turn it into a
whole number from 1 to 100:<P>
<PRE>
Math.floor(Math.random() * 100) + 1
</PRE>
<CODE>Math.random() * 100</CODE> is between 0 and 99.999..., <CODE>Math.floor</CODE> rounds it down to a
whole number from 0 to 99, and adding 1 gives 1 to 100.<P>
<H3>A guessing game</H3>
<PRE>
// Guess - guess the number I'm thinking of
const secret = Math.floor(Math.random() * 100) + 1;
let guesses = 0;

print(&quot;I'm thinking of a number from 1 to 100.&quot;);
while (true) {
  const guess = Number(await input('Your guess: '));
  guesses = guesses + 1;
  if (guess === secret) {
    print(`Right! You took ${guesses} guesses.`);
    break;
  } else if (guess &lt; secret) {
    print('Too low.');
  } else {
    print('Too high.');
  }
}
</PRE>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/Guess" WIDTH=586 HEIGHT=349></CENTER>
<CODE>while (true)</CODE> looks as though it would go round for ever, since <CODE>true</CODE> is always
true. The way out is <CODE>break</CODE>, which stops a loop immediately and carries on after it. Here that
happens when you guess the number. <CODE>break</CODE> works in <CODE>for</CODE> loops too.<P>
The program counts your guesses in <CODE>guesses</CODE>, which starts at 0 and has one added each time
round.<P>
<B>Warning</B>: a loop that never ends, and never waits for you to type something, stops the whole desktop:
nothing else can run until it finishes, and it never does. If this happens, reload the page in your web
browser. Anything you have saved is safe on the hard disc, but save your program before running it, in case.
For example, this loop never ends, because <CODE>i</CODE> is never changed:<P>
<PRE>
let i = 1;
while (i &lt;= 10) {
  print(i);
}
</PRE>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>Summary</H2>
<UL>
<LI><CODE>true</CODE> and <CODE>false</CODE> are booleans. Comparisons (<CODE>===</CODE>,
<CODE>!==</CODE>, <CODE>&lt;</CODE>, <CODE>&gt;</CODE>, <CODE>&lt;=</CODE>, <CODE>&gt;=</CODE>) give a
boolean. Use <CODE>===</CODE>, not <CODE>==</CODE>.<P>
<LI><CODE>&amp;&amp;</CODE> (and), <CODE>||</CODE> (or) and <CODE>!</CODE> (not) combine them.<P>
<LI><CODE>if (...) { ... } else if (...) { ... } else { ... }</CODE> runs the first block whose comparison
is true.<P>
<LI><CODE>for (let i = 1; i &lt;= 10; i++) { ... }</CODE> repeats a block with a counter;
<CODE>while (...) { ... }</CODE> repeats it for as long as something is true. <CODE>break</CODE> leaves a
loop early.<P>
<LI><CODE>Math.floor(Math.random() * n) + 1</CODE> is a random whole number from 1 to n.<P>
<LI>A variable made inside a block only exists inside that block.<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S7></A>
<H2>Exercises</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Write a program that asks for a number and says whether it is odd or even.<P>
<DT><DD>2  Change Tables so that it asks how far to go, and prints the table up to that number instead of
up to 12.<P>
<DT><DD>3  Write a program that prints the numbers from 10 down to 1 and then <CODE>Lift off!</CODE>. (A
<CODE>for</CODE> loop can count down: <CODE>i--</CODE> subtracts one.)<P>
<DT><DD>4  Change Guess so that you only have seven guesses. If you run out, it should tell you what the
number was.<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S8></A>
<H2>Answers</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  A number is even when the remainder after dividing by 2 is 0:
<PRE>
const n = Number(await input('A whole number: '));
if (n % 2 === 0) {
  print(`${n} is even.`);
} else {
  print(`${n} is odd.`);
}
</PRE>
<DT><DD>2  Ask a second question and use the answer in the loop's comparison:
<PRE>
const last = Number(await input('Up to? '));
for (let i = 1; i &lt;= last; i++) {
</PRE>
<DT><DD>3
<PRE>
for (let i = 10; i &gt;= 1; i--) {
  print(i);
}
print('Lift off!');
</PRE>
<DT><DD>4  Change the loop to a <CODE>for</CODE> loop that goes round at most seven times, and check
afterwards whether the last guess was right. The full program is <CODE>GuessAns4</CODE> in the examples
directory. Notice that <CODE>guess</CODE> is made with <CODE>let</CODE> before the loop, not inside it: the
check after the loop needs it, and a variable made inside the loop's curly brackets only exists inside
them.
<PRE>
// GuessAns4 - guess the number, in seven guesses or fewer
const secret = Math.floor(Math.random() * 100) + 1;
let guess = 0;

print(&quot;I'm thinking of a number from 1 to 100.&quot;);
for (let guesses = 1; guesses &lt;= 7; guesses++) {
  guess = Number(await input(`Guess ${guesses} of 7: `));
  if (guess === secret) {
    print(`Right! You took ${guesses} guesses.`);
    break;
  } else if (guess &lt; secret) {
    print('Too low.');
  } else {
    print('Too high.');
  }
}

if (guess !== secret) {
  print(`Out of guesses! It was ${secret}.`);
}
</PRE>
</DL>

